### “File” in C – A Quick Overview

In C, a **file** is simply a persistent stream of bytes stored on some storage device (hard disk, SSD, USB stick, etc.).  
C gives you two main ways to work with files:

| Level | What you get | Typical API |
|-------|--------------|-------------|
| **Low‑level** | File descriptor (an integer) | `open()`, `read()`, `write()`, `close()` (POSIX) |
| **High‑level** | `FILE *` stream | `fopen()`, `fread()`, `fwrite()`, `fclose()`, `fprintf()`, `fscanf()`, … |

Below we’ll focus on the **standard I/O (`stdio.h`)** interface because it’s portable across Windows, Linux, macOS, etc.

---

## 1. Opening a File

```c
#include <stdio.h>

FILE *fp = fopen("data.txt", "r");   // open for reading
if (!fp) {
    perror("fopen");
    return 1;
}
```

### Mode strings

| Mode | Meaning | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| `"r"` | Read only, file must exist | `fopen("file.txt","r")` |
| `"w"` | Write only, truncate or create | `fopen("file.txt","w")` |
| `"a"` | Append, create if missing | `fopen("file.txt","a")` |
| `"r+"` | Read/write, file must exist | `fopen("file.txt","r+")` |
| `"w+"` | Read/write, truncate or create | `fopen("file.txt","w+")` |
| `"a+"` | Read/append, create if missing | `fopen("file.txt","a+")` |
| Add `b` for binary mode (Windows only) | `"rb"`, `"wb"`, … | `fopen("image.bin","rb")` |

> **Tip:** On Windows, text mode (`"r"`) translates `\r\n` to `\n` on input and `\n` to `\r\n` on output. Binary mode (`"rb"`) disables this translation.

---

## 2. Reading & Writing

| Function | Purpose | Example |
|----------|---------|---------|
| `fread()` | Read raw bytes | `size_t n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof(buf), fp);` |
| `fwrite()` | Write raw bytes | `size_t n = fwrite(buf, 1, sizeof(buf), fp);` |
| `fgetc()` | Read one character | `int c = fgetc(fp);` |
| `fputc()` | Write one character | `fputc('A', fp);` |
| `fgets()` | Read a line (text mode) | `char line[128]; fgets(line, sizeof line, fp);` |
| `fputs()` | Write a string (text mode) | `fputs("Hello\n", fp);` |
| `fprintf()` | Formatted output | `fprintf(fp, "%d %s\n", 42, "foo");` |
| `fscanf()` | Formatted input | `int x; fscanf(fp, "%d", &x);` |

### Example: Copy a file

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int copy_file(const char *src, const char *dst) {
    FILE *in  = fopen(src, "rb");
    FILE *out = fopen(dst, "wb");
    if (!in || !out) return -1;

    char buf[4096];
    size_t n;
    while ((n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, in)) > 0) {
        if (fwrite(buf, 1, n, out) != n) {
            perror("fwrite");
            break;
        }
    }

    fclose(in);
    fclose(out);
    return 0;
}
```

---

## 3. Seeking & Positioning

| Function | Purpose | Example |
|----------|---------|---------|
| `fseek()` | Move file pointer | `fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_END);` |
| `ftell()` | Get current position | `long pos = ftell(fp);` |
| `rewind()` | Move to start (equivalent to `fseek(fp,0,SEEK_SET)`) | `rewind(fp);` |

> **Note:** `ftell()` returns a `long`. On large files you may need `ftello()`/`fseeko()` (POSIX) or `_ftelli64()` on Windows.

---

## 4. Closing a File

```c
if (fclose(fp) != 0) {
    perror("fclose");
}
```

`fclose()` flushes any buffered output and releases the underlying file descriptor.

---

## 5. Error Handling

| Function | What it tells you | Typical use |
|----------|-------------------|-------------|
| `feof(fp)` | End‑of‑file reached | `while (!feof(fp)) { ... }` |
| `ferror(fp)` | Read/write error | `if (ferror(fp)) perror("file error");` |
| `perror()` | Print last error | `perror("fopen");` |

---

## 6. Low‑Level (POSIX) Alternative

If you need finer control (e.g., non‑blocking I/O, file locking, permissions), use the POSIX API:

```c
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int fd = open("file.txt", O_RDONLY);
ssize_t n = read(fd, buf, sizeof buf);
close(fd);
```

On Windows, the equivalent is `CreateFile()`, `ReadFile()`, `WriteFile()`, `CloseHandle()`.

---

## 7. Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| Forgetting to close a file | Resource leak | Always call `fclose()` (or `close()`) |
| Mixing text and binary modes | Unexpected newline translation | Use `"rb"`/`"wb"` consistently |
| Using `fgets()` without checking for `NULL` | Buffer overflow or crash | Check return value |
| Assuming `fread()` reads the requested number of bytes | May read fewer on EOF or error | Loop until desired count or `feof()`/`ferror()` |
| Using `ftell()` on a file larger than 2 GB | `long` overflow | Use `ftello()`/`fseeko()` or platform‑specific 64‑bit APIs |

---

## 8. Quick Reference Cheat Sheet

```c
// Open
FILE *fp = fopen("file.txt", "r");

// Read
char buf[256];
size_t n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, fp);

// Write
fwrite(buf, 1, n, fp);

// Text helpers
fgets(buf, sizeof buf, fp);
fputs(buf, fp);

// Formatted
fprintf(fp, "%d %s\n", 42, "hello");
fscanf(fp, "%d", &x);

// Seek
fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_SET);   // start
fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_END);   // end
long pos = ftell(fp);

// Close
fclose(fp);
```

---

### Bottom Line

In C, a **file** is a stream of bytes you can open, read from, write to, and close. The standard library (`stdio.h`) gives you a convenient, portable set of functions (`fopen`, `fread`, `fwrite`, `fclose`, etc.) that hide the underlying OS details. For more advanced control, fall back to the low‑level POSIX or Windows APIs. Happy coding!
